#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb008515-d668-509b-8c8a-76e892e709cc" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12cdf423-e581-53c9-b943-12d52221b2a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387250} {:answer/id #uuid "b9522f90-63cc-5efe-a46b-2246bf7d7b2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387251} {:answer/id #uuid "eb39fe71-c1b3-593f-b630-c516e6f8448a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387252} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa89b78-e5fc-5030-ac4f-50357fc840b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387253} {:answer/id #uuid "47e4606a-831f-5f42-a986-6462e2d1edbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9522f90-63cc-5efe-a46b-2246bf7d7b2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387251} :answer-field/id #uuid "87e951f7-7453-52d8-b44c-a2ad3dc52dee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b42e9373-4893-5f93-a4eb-1c63f18b5e00" :question/math-academy-id "q-122588" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\,\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,-3)$ to the point $(3,0)$ in the fourth quadrant?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](images/b7/b7c2c6f9e50988629007a06213cb3acf0a48f9951f026fb70d5a986b760abdfe.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\,\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{9-(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{9-9{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }9{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387255 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb008515-d668-509b-8c8a-76e892e709cc" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fa67403-3c4c-5d88-b227-4c700c3a7dd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387257} {:answer/id #uuid "3091951d-4759-5af3-94ce-b0eebddefa0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387258} {:answer/id #uuid "2c830dfe-8d7e-5efc-b4c4-c776aeed527f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387259} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8dfc76-d958-59a5-990c-23331e92b469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387260} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e5108e-fb63-5987-9568-84f090a3978a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9e5108e-fb63-5987-9568-84f090a3978a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387261} :answer-field/id #uuid "78075364-c535-58e8-b896-df95c6542768" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a882cfb-b3f3-519e-9b6e-6e0eebe95df2" :question/math-academy-id "q-122591" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(-2,0)$ to the point $(0,-2)$ in the third quadrant?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](images/1f/1fd6c2df8b8467a46ed36b515842b88656883733376ee732839bab4085ca019c.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =8{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}8{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd5c5fd8-409c-5b92-a0f1-4466c76cdbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbd8e3fe-9258-5fd4-ac38-46ac64db6bc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387264} {:answer/id #uuid "da8671ca-6c00-5ad9-a9e3-7c2197ed1890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2y{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387265} {:answer/id #uuid "e5372737-b3d4-5fdf-b4fd-fc53a2121ec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387266} {:answer/id #uuid "0bcb5dce-0d72-5149-b95d-b553c17f8c50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-2y)\\,i+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387267} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e00fb2-b549-5885-b25c-39af3314dd26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4e00fb2-b549-5885-b25c-39af3314dd26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387268} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1c2ee55-5d94-5079-b34c-8536d3fd9a20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9b6323d-ff08-50ce-be1a-fa1f61c66864" :question/math-academy-id "q-122608" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y)) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y)) \\\\  & =-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a43f82e-6682-56af-994a-b0bdf12b4095" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c7d1d36-652e-5181-9302-abbb5df8798b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/1461b0737bf83fee8e16c1e5e102c36c2042e46935550ebc08a0d5774a561cd3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387271} {:answer/id #uuid "293009e5-bb46-5343-920e-66801c6cfbce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ac/acb2066428e0660e7d4e106acd5faf60600d6f93de0b6cf4c43ee2b5864e6e51.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387272} {:answer/id #uuid "7be00e9c-596e-5c63-ac00-60deba93c44a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0d/0d5f1fb1b13459937f7bd3ff0ed8b6da641fcd346dc961d7ec5b98be29e45b1d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387273} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9fdbaf-e58e-56ff-981f-39b0969f23ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cd/cdebaf3d8c07fe6cb26c4fb2c5b72d2bd60dc968b3345fc1c4a5737d8c6c2b53.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387274} {:answer/id #uuid "e227d23f-1359-5012-8c53-e4feec2f7c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8a/8a35641fa57b01a95a0c1d5b3dba8a0072b291a6822ac7b2f5e0473b9037796b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c7d1d36-652e-5181-9302-abbb5df8798b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/1461b0737bf83fee8e16c1e5e102c36c2042e46935550ebc08a0d5774a561cd3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387271} :answer-field/id #uuid "07a54f60-4401-5cf2-ba14-b1330ea47fc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40689dc7-e924-59ea-9bdd-9f2acdedd9ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-122619" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i+2y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-4,1),$ $(1,1),$ and $(3,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-4,1) & =i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,-1) & =i+2\\cdot (-1)\\,j=i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/14/1461b0737bf83fee8e16c1e5e102c36c2042e46935550ebc08a0d5774a561cd3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a43f82e-6682-56af-994a-b0bdf12b4095" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bec373fa-27cc-5734-871a-ec77749ea6a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0c/0c425b6fcb88f4c958a7c0371a64a815eda5ebf8ed14b0adc06875de1e24003b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387278} {:answer/id #uuid "6120694d-f5d6-5a30-a9b1-04836470b4fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/af7fdf48d36f477174867761b90d9349344029980012f1f7907f544efd3893bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387279} {:answer/id #uuid "5738a2a6-58f2-5cb8-befa-f76d6d0fb2c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/48/48c57f24904442c74178682db34aaa0113eb84042c78516a4e6f961e73b0f9eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387280} {:answer/id #uuid "11eee978-2cbc-57da-8a9a-9ffddf6422c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/02/020c3ec7cd0e5e57bb4c6de8516988187c9038d9e5598b116966b71fa8b917d8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387281} {:answer/id #uuid "04551b05-601c-557a-a5a2-69cba4463197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/25/25ed4b4750662433bf4405b5e63ab78396688a985c76e2d34d449c0689830380.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11eee978-2cbc-57da-8a9a-9ffddf6422c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/02/020c3ec7cd0e5e57bb4c6de8516988187c9038d9e5598b116966b71fa8b917d8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387281} :answer-field/id #uuid "c39f6b60-6db1-56ec-93e1-e634b438a208" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14b7ceda-a408-53c9-aeb5-05da06c84fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-122732" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(2,1),$ and $(-2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & ={1}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1\\,j=i-4j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & ={1}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-1) & =(-1)^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-1)\\,j=i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/02/020c3ec7cd0e5e57bb4c6de8516988187c9038d9e5598b116966b71fa8b917d8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387283 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b00f0c-fc7f-5262-b1a3-cdd6c1560cd6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de1513c2-3155-5def-a5b5-5b833a833652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b6/b6a7c1faba2bb5d18d5f729d2576d7475f6ce1784698fae0a1c1ea97b80d6180.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387285} {:answer/id #uuid "f9bf0d9a-d009-5dbb-8f91-ece881c3e02c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d44395fa166c654c41322bd31e1a9bf9bc6c7a398e27da7f9de9d249ccdb1107.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387286} {:answer/id #uuid "0b6544d5-cd56-547b-895e-df2376403680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8c/8cdc92839252560ad52b54bb77d9db75ba066e33bfa5a3c451e4cbb6345e9d33.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387287} {:answer/id #uuid "8bade9cb-3b17-5e2f-ab51-5cbf5f1198f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/25/2560beb4dcff267890c47ad5df8ac7aef0e5de64a57384f8c71e35d8325c892b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387288} {:answer/id #uuid "45a0eeab-6f8a-5b04-8150-613eb8858a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8ec9af02a374c1b8a954117ad055f78e9e2ac74bc391bb230968198a3a474fef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45a0eeab-6f8a-5b04-8150-613eb8858a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8ec9af02a374c1b8a954117ad055f78e9e2ac74bc391bb230968198a3a474fef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387289} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc3d49d5-a5f3-575b-83f9-afd438ed4ffd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e446a55-b0d3-59f4-abde-52af19baebe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-122745" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{y}{xy+1}\\,i+\\frac{x}{xy+1}\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,0),(1,-2),$ and $(0,2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=\\frac{y}{xy+1}\\,i+\\frac{x}{xy+1}\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-2,0) & =\\frac{0}{(-2)(0)+1}\\,i+\\frac{-2}{(-2)(0)+1}\\,j=-2\\,j \\\\ F(1,-2) & =\\frac{-2}{(1)(-2)+1}\\,i+\\frac{1}{(1)(-2)+1}\\,j=2\\,i-j \\\\ F(0,2) & =\\frac{2}{(0)(2)+1}\\,i+\\frac{0}{(0)(2)+1}\\,j=2\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/8e/8ec9af02a374c1b8a954117ad055f78e9e2ac74bc391bb230968198a3a474fef.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b00f0c-fc7f-5262-b1a3-cdd6c1560cd6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9a9e026-4bc7-575e-be23-f281b7660b3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d688cdc804635688df6780bfb674622a491528c85802397cc3dccb6bd4a2d6be.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387292} {:answer/id #uuid "60880678-2b0d-57f7-8c8d-ae95a3c4b0f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1f/1f597a7886abe6a8cddca9192658c8ded57dd96b010dfbf166e8cea6484872e7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387293} {:answer/id #uuid "71e0ba0c-ade1-5e87-9ac1-d66c6f763265" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/11/114f65bec341c3bef23ffda186b7d5a721e89e607d15c64624b6145e734c68c0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387294} {:answer/id #uuid "4150b677-4ab5-5642-97f3-e462d77d1b78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8b20800589467fa460547519312ed283d373ce2b7553b0c397413799b2d6597f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387295} {:answer/id #uuid "bcafb174-7327-5bc3-a3a6-55a1c3530cdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/53/5317b575c101e4a782e17f9f83f56f66f8151a1d1c12cd0084020429435ba542.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9a9e026-4bc7-575e-be23-f281b7660b3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d688cdc804635688df6780bfb674622a491528c85802397cc3dccb6bd4a2d6be.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387292} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb7e4ea8-2187-5984-93cd-b8162a3b3097" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "709ee56e-badf-53e5-8fe9-cd6e97119ca1" :question/math-academy-id "q-122752" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=(y-2x)\\,i+(2y-4x)\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,3),(-1,-1),$ and $(3,2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=(y-2x)\\,i+(2y-4x)\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,3) & =(3-2(1))\\,i+(2(3)-4(1))\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ F(-1,-1) & =(-1-2(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)-4(-1))\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ F(3,2) & =(2-2(3))\\,i+(2(2)-4(3))\\,j=-4\\,i-8\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/d6/d688cdc804635688df6780bfb674622a491528c85802397cc3dccb6bd4a2d6be.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b00f0c-fc7f-5262-b1a3-cdd6c1560cd6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d40339cb-9f8c-56b1-9446-2bb79c2ae322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4c/4c9fc6ae3cbbf6fa3d241d3607b26fee1cd0670fde37494638ca90da2b02ecfa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387299} {:answer/id #uuid "3b36441e-f03d-5f2c-b78a-49b57116a3cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d6084f0711f728a09a78c9803bec9c6e54274d6d72a2adc2b1547651e0ec702c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387300} {:answer/id #uuid "116d6c88-d5ff-5163-b1bc-a148e6194baf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3e/3e9efea8998b1dbaab3cc6d89dce4f99bdb06066e81fcb78a2b3a0e412e2e44c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387301} {:answer/id #uuid "e73588d4-0678-5e1c-aac4-ffad61b46f23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/80/80ecb3a560bc23359c3b44baace0523bfe653f0c5a5fee5b5c646d901ade5feb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387302} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c0c612-25c2-5765-8c88-fa1c6a482813" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/040d62d956945b7bb67cae560cda4e09a22be2fa9cd2e5da3f3cc372269f14e4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b36441e-f03d-5f2c-b78a-49b57116a3cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d6084f0711f728a09a78c9803bec9c6e54274d6d72a2adc2b1547651e0ec702c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387300} :answer-field/id #uuid "c92e3f54-7df7-528f-9e7f-fbd995c31ee0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b5b3650-421e-53e6-b6b4-9b4c7a243072" :question/math-academy-id "q-122754" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=(y-2)\\,i+(x+2)\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-5,3),(0,0),$ and $(2,-3).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=(y-2)\\,i+(x+2)\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-5,3) & =(3-2)\\,i+(-5+2)\\,j=i-3\\,j \\\\ F(0,0) & =(0-2)\\,i+(0+2)\\,j=-2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(2,-3) & =(-3-2)\\,i+(2+2)\\,j=-5\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/d6/d6084f0711f728a09a78c9803bec9c6e54274d6d72a2adc2b1547651e0ec702c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a43f82e-6682-56af-994a-b0bdf12b4095" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba90d78-1161-52a4-93c0-d995aaaf7e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6c/6cf967e8a5d3bc7b2ac021414875f5221ef52790dfbdd6e214cccc7f0e8be2d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387305} {:answer/id #uuid "bb982230-23d8-541a-a311-793fcd335e70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4b/4be9586aaa886ccfe092f93c106a5f7233509c798c9a90ecfda0363173dc7afb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387306} {:answer/id #uuid "6f167c07-25dc-5f9e-956f-2fe0933e7ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/95/9593ba00b964194c1d91549fe38a1334a478ffd6b724534c4512a11d19d683aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387307} {:answer/id #uuid "640a700e-3b7c-59e8-83e6-b88bdc953d51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f8/f814f8c7e6d879cbe25e5108704e29464d0c39859371ee4fac3bfb3e72f90696.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387308} {:answer/id #uuid "09b9b72e-d802-5315-b38d-ee456018cf2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/26/26a5c0123d93231a800456e9f06f6ba7450861838c87d4d1b3ae77dab4cec6cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09b9b72e-d802-5315-b38d-ee456018cf2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/26/26a5c0123d93231a800456e9f06f6ba7450861838c87d4d1b3ae77dab4cec6cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387309} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7fe3fc1-2c7d-5b68-96b7-12857eae01aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe3ebbef-9b0f-50cb-ab18-6df5f7b1d83c" :question/math-academy-id "q-122789" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}\\,i-y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,2),$ $(2,-3),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,2) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (-2)^{2}\\,i-2\\,j=3\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-3) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-(-3)\\,j=3\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-\\,j=3\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/26/26a5c0123d93231a800456e9f06f6ba7450861838c87d4d1b3ae77dab4cec6cf.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "364d2c9a-6e9a-57f8-aa57-c10ee223061b" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2280c113-775e-55c4-a01a-374b591fe98d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b8/b8b4cae5128667b4a75097540bddc8dbb853744aee65e96204cf630ccb30fc68.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387312} {:answer/id #uuid "62456e8c-d83a-57a0-89bf-f27036a5952d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6598c50e72417a0bff5514fa2b8ff9fba7c617cff10dc83e15472259d040846.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387313} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7deb3d-fec1-58c8-a1ac-06e1e23abd55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387314} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9c1117-85a8-52e1-8899-dd286a49cfd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8e30bba74fad98713f891950fd052d2ddd2cb2dc349bd9bcb7efe3333b7203dd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387315} {:answer/id #uuid "244086f1-424e-529c-87bd-716006661658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dc/dcd7de7b52c59ceda71245ef18d7cb1327a0e3f3b93f6f8e0bb8c5e386958217.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2280c113-775e-55c4-a01a-374b591fe98d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b8/b8b4cae5128667b4a75097540bddc8dbb853744aee65e96204cf630ccb30fc68.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387312} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7d6acf-0c52-544c-acec-75dbce89c666" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84a12606-a26d-58d9-96bf-2e993f7dda01" :question/math-academy-id "q-122813" :question/problem "![](images/50/50bd80ab9aa2f8d5ac2a453ccfe63815a75379efa7502cf1090eb015264206f6.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/b7/b73b05cb7f58bcce1d684c7f19c391ecd2033eb4c47c59daf896546956a9d42f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/b8/b8b4cae5128667b4a75097540bddc8dbb853744aee65e96204cf630ccb30fc68.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "364d2c9a-6e9a-57f8-aa57-c10ee223061b" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da3c3d0-ffca-58b0-9973-21ca78efe966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/93/9355e5a989137d0d74ee803bf0fb0248fc68e4df4aa1843afede93d1bb506a45.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387319} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6c888d-3419-51b8-8e63-8b6f80586e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/00/00b722a09fef4428d17fc7ff6a301b3860e798b20c44126b63cd381ad2884398.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387320} {:answer/id #uuid "522cd20b-1e88-577f-9897-b7d808dced3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ce/cecc7f28c20523aac9f153022d64da4233ce995e05df3b3e2886b7fb3b20d089.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387321} {:answer/id #uuid "7370d0a5-e8d6-5b38-af6b-5d647d33fadb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8e30bba74fad98713f891950fd052d2ddd2cb2dc349bd9bcb7efe3333b7203dd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387322} {:answer/id #uuid "da9ecd53-f00c-59aa-9818-8a0bb184f65d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "522cd20b-1e88-577f-9897-b7d808dced3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ce/cecc7f28c20523aac9f153022d64da4233ce995e05df3b3e2886b7fb3b20d089.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387321} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c14dfe7-e6c0-5526-b094-6b4a396050e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70739974-680b-512e-bb5a-c7c80411f351" :question/math-academy-id "q-122838" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7dfad4e44184f68de36d455e99895fce8c879d4b053c06aa5805c423bdb1bfc.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/9f/9f22df07aac0933106381dce4571e674d656428bf7a976e16bed6d46898f814f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/ce/cecc7f28c20523aac9f153022d64da4233ce995e05df3b3e2886b7fb3b20d089.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c89778fa-3c8d-5f0a-a0f8-c9b3d9193ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using an Inverse Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a82d5f6-66f3-5522-a100-e2f8dae13966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387326} {:answer/id #uuid "629adac7-7e11-5b16-8d75-97adcdbde8b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387327} {:answer/id #uuid "1bbc0dc2-d7b9-50b2-a002-118c594c07e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387328} {:answer/id #uuid "08ad482e-16a9-5965-ad6e-397324dce8bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387329} {:answer/id #uuid "6be35ef2-6065-55cf-b015-361f21dd7ef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a82d5f6-66f3-5522-a100-e2f8dae13966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387326} :answer-field/id #uuid "299f6ec7-a02a-58c0-aab8-a90cf14d427a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33942b97-95c2-5a05-bee1-52e88505e10f" :question/math-academy-id "q-12284" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x-2y=3 \\\\ 2x-y=5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "440cb768-be67-5dd3-98c8-6a58f060245c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c501b668-1a8b-52b8-bfbc-0865e77b89ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+45}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387333} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8e09de-8241-5d41-a528-d43d0b83321f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387334} {:answer/id #uuid "5284dbe0-5be2-5274-935e-78522341abb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387335} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce5154b-bdf7-5036-86b6-48af2c151a19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387336} {:answer/id #uuid "8e19472f-2116-55fd-ae86-a1dc8bf1dbe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+45}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ce5154b-bdf7-5036-86b6-48af2c151a19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387336} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e06052-07fc-5a61-a1f2-4c109fa7c59b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fb44864-edbc-5a3d-bf6d-c1269d74e67b" :question/math-academy-id "q-123143" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{2x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+49{y}^{2}=196,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{7}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $7$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=7\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{2x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }7\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "440cb768-be67-5dd3-98c8-6a58f060245c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed86b67b-d5cb-57b5-a24d-29a4d73c30c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387340} {:answer/id #uuid "efa48bfc-8b2b-5a22-b35e-60ccac71d522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387341} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3ea36b-e9a3-5562-a6ed-053fcefda30c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387342} {:answer/id #uuid "083f15a4-94ea-5c6f-aefc-cee09c65a61f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387343} {:answer/id #uuid "12515b53-de19-592f-88a4-ab9e66663875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed86b67b-d5cb-57b5-a24d-29a4d73c30c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387340} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fa2a43b-c17a-52ce-9fb0-1b82291a1d31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae604189-87b2-5cc4-80d1-9a191fe6507f" :question/math-academy-id "q-123146" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}2x\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=64,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12{sin}^{2}⁡t+4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12{sin}^{2}⁡t+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "440cb768-be67-5dd3-98c8-6a58f060245c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cd2be39-2852-5a3b-b0b5-50515fa9399a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387347} {:answer/id #uuid "18298c3e-ac12-5ad3-8b8d-c7251a53b2da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387348} {:answer/id #uuid "33e46136-ccb1-5344-bca2-73c7389b9c07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387349} {:answer/id #uuid "32ae8c65-c6e7-5a6d-9e54-0fc3c2b9d30b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{3{cos}^{2}⁡t+9}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387350} {:answer/id #uuid "cb50e43b-2bff-5fbb-acde-eb728a7c598f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33e46136-ccb1-5344-bca2-73c7389b9c07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387349} :answer-field/id #uuid "169b9432-e996-59fd-8a2c-3051df7da254" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56d45ff5-8d46-5d91-ada0-7afe1d0328af" :question/math-academy-id "q-123153" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(3-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the ellipse $12{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=36,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $\\sqrt{3}$ and $2\\sqrt{3},$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+12{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+3({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3-{x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3-(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =3-3{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =3(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(3-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f0e2596-73d6-540c-af84-b19a943da6a8" :knowledge-point/title "The Second Derivative of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c4f3a69-92f8-593d-a5d5-10afbe7a1536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)({tan}^{2}⁡(t)-{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387354} {:answer/id #uuid "59ff0643-9755-5655-9e7a-c6a58bcc6ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)({tan}^{2}⁡t+{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)),\\,-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387355} {:answer/id #uuid "d4dbbc4f-0523-5ed7-98a6-d97ffa12a2b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)),\\,-\\frac{3}{t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387356} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3ab8fd-29e4-55fd-ab10-4110925db260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)({tan}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)),\\,-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387357} {:answer/id #uuid "83af61a3-eb17-5200-919f-b41643d2b386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)({tan}^{2}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc3ab8fd-29e4-55fd-ab10-4110925db260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)({tan}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)),\\,-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387357} :answer-field/id #uuid "21580012-8c25-5076-900b-45885ca5b66a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b02b05ec-3c49-5ba7-a525-3faa0181bc7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-123184" :question/problem "Given that $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{3})⟩,$ ${r}^{″}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the second derivative of a vector-valued function, we find the second derivatives of the components.\n\n- For $x(t)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t) \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t){tan}^{2}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t){sec}^{2}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)({tan}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{3}{t} \\\\ {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨{x}^{″}(t),\\,{y}^{″}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)({tan}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sec}^{2}⁡(2t)),\\,-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ccd52-63db-5d28-b78b-2df7caf52ad0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b97203e9-8d87-5cac-b645-904ef91cae7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,\\,\\frac{3}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387361} {:answer/id #uuid "4f63b47e-4384-5932-bde6-b16ca9aebd87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,\\frac{3}{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387362} {:answer/id #uuid "5941fefb-b666-5264-9ff1-ea630cf1bd88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨12,\\,\\frac{1}{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387363} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8b17a6-4cbf-54f9-99c3-f7a62dd1829d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,\\,\\frac{4}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387364} {:answer/id #uuid "53d7fc04-e503-5637-99f3-3e1a31b96eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{3}{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f63b47e-4384-5932-bde6-b16ca9aebd87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,\\frac{3}{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387362} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7722d9c-8a8e-51c2-9846-c98ae9635908" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12003e8d-c48c-5642-87cc-c7c437d424fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-123203" :question/problem "Given that $r(t)=⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\,{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)⟩,$ calculate $r(\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into our function, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}),\\,{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,\\frac{3}{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08dc697e-fd67-5ae0-889b-ea81c69e2277" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a93738ab-9081-5c6f-8511-86ef418346d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{3{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387368} {:answer/id #uuid "16d6b119-3bc3-5a27-9e7b-17462013838f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{3{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387369} {:answer/id #uuid "61985336-57b3-5c35-af3b-a7c51ff9e8e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387370} {:answer/id #uuid "52078fb4-b647-50ef-98c9-e3fb6359dbe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387371} {:answer/id #uuid "11b75cb3-33d2-5ac2-98e1-7e293e54d2e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11b75cb3-33d2-5ac2-98e1-7e293e54d2e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387372} :answer-field/id #uuid "77cf4060-1d63-5c4b-b8af-00a20dc038f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "093d0403-9b58-520d-b909-bbab14fea8b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-123207" :question/problem "![](images/7f/7fc64a261de4941b5ad35b8308b82b0f51c6b6bfbde177aafd793a0a0ac99da6.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the quarter of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=12,$ located in the second quadrant, shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $\\sqrt{3}$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+3({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9653f49-1a01-5acd-8ce6-f3cd8095fdbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{7t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387375} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3322db-d223-55b3-b7dc-f9fd758349fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387376} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c61a6c-a91d-50a4-a701-bf4e530a3094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387377} {:answer/id #uuid "d4be85c4-9c1a-5af1-b31a-957bb0b77474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387378} {:answer/id #uuid "95f050db-1db6-5212-8a8b-3de82028eac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{7t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4be85c4-9c1a-5af1-b31a-957bb0b77474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387378} :answer-field/id #uuid "e77fad7f-b6e3-5827-9df5-363a99a7be9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2a142a9-d042-56b2-ba86-b30a7fb0dfd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-123283" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,4)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=-3\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t(b) \\\\  & =t(-3\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-3t\\,i+4t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-3t$ and $y=4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)j \\\\  & =-3\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{-3t+4t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08dc697e-fd67-5ae0-889b-ea81c69e2277" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45471587-a7b6-5b1b-b719-d3191acd9214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387382} {:answer/id #uuid "71fd8321-f9a6-51e7-bfa3-7cfd909174cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{36{cos}^{2}⁡t+11}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387383} {:answer/id #uuid "e34c6854-26cf-5803-b417-8b39a6025039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387384} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1b61e2-d4d6-56cf-9d31-54bbcd9c6297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387385} {:answer/id #uuid "96bdd232-9416-51e7-8540-73037f2f89d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{36{cos}^{2}⁡t+11}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e34c6854-26cf-5803-b417-8b39a6025039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387384} :answer-field/id #uuid "60c78d9a-0398-5bca-9a48-b162d6138619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d67c9ae-1059-5bb6-951e-c7139ab21d55" :question/math-academy-id "q-123300" :question/problem "![](images/34/34b4b349b8295ee6443a85f019f6bf5e7ea6c6aafe9109ac7935610e6aefcacf.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}(25-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is three-quarters of the ellipse $36{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=900,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{6}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $5$ and $6,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡t+36{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=25-{x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =25-(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =25-25{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =25(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =25{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(25-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }25{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =25{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14ab317b-fd33-5d6e-9d54-87e749b78283" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Along Segments in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e2e9acc-3c64-51a7-9ab5-d350f9449ab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387389} {:answer/id #uuid "89ac3f7c-fb26-5086-aea8-2db3445e4653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387390} {:answer/id #uuid "ef97d2f5-fde9-5e22-82b7-c60bd3fea467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{t-1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387391} {:answer/id #uuid "ec794748-a3c8-58b6-8929-a9cf739d2848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{5t-3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387392} {:answer/id #uuid "26182461-9d9e-569f-b647-6e1cb1f93a87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26182461-9d9e-569f-b647-6e1cb1f93a87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387393} :answer-field/id #uuid "36154aca-9593-55ae-8c89-27fef1f72807" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "963025de-8b37-535e-a08b-0b16983c48ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-123387" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z},$ traversed along the line segment from $(3,-2,3)$ to $(5,-1,7)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(3,-2,3)$ to $(5,-1,7).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=3i-2\\,j+3k$ and $b=5\\,i-j+7\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(3i-2\\,j+3k)+t[(5\\,i-j+7\\,k)-(3i-2\\,j+3k)] \\\\  & =(3i-2\\,j+3k)+t(2i+\\,j+4k) \\\\  & =(2t+3)\\,i+(t-2)\\,j+(4t+3)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t+3,y=t-2,$ and $z=4t+3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-2)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+3)k \\\\  & =2i+j+4\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{2(2t+3)+(t-2)-(4t+3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\cdot \\sqrt{21}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53fca396-4ade-5180-9c9b-a73f24f5b5b8" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c6a8d34-daca-5bd1-b562-af1cacd3ffe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{t+1},\\,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387396} {:answer/id #uuid "9b23d2d4-ecb6-5869-a8a3-795ba6afc693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{3t},\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387397} {:answer/id #uuid "fa484598-cbf3-571e-9dec-073f5cfeabb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{t+1},\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387398} {:answer/id #uuid "1ae1f92f-7f8a-5559-a71b-4fa8507ef3cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{t-1},\\,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387399} {:answer/id #uuid "77ede2a1-af5b-50f4-9c95-82da057e58bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{t},\\,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c6a8d34-daca-5bd1-b562-af1cacd3ffe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{t+1},\\,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387396} :answer-field/id #uuid "340494bd-bfd3-5c54-a91d-b093bd1e75c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae9740d6-44f0-50b4-8094-5aec220611bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-123435" :question/problem "Find $\\int ⟨{e}^{t+1},\\,12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{e}^{t+1},\\,12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)⟩\\text{d}t=⟨\\int {e}^{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x(t)={e}^{t+1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t+1}\\text{d}t={e}^{t+1}+{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t)=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ we calculate its integral using substitution. So, we let $u=3t.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 12\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{3}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)+{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{e}^{t+1},\\,12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨{e}^{t+1}+{c}_{1},\\,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t+1},\\,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t+1},\\,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "973cf948-eeb3-5c46-b0ac-ee0307a8043a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals of Unity Over Polar Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfa492a8-e07a-522a-bf45-34af18c7a267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{2} θ\\tan^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168604} {:answer/id #uuid "1813a2d0-d5bd-52b4-99b5-efcb1d2a7640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + \\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168605} {:answer/id #uuid "2aba48aa-c769-5c6a-9e07-f0c67be25a4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + 9\\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168606} {:answer/id #uuid "55f60188-6938-5f92-aae8-dedef3328cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{9 + \\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168607} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3a3ab0-69fc-59cc-bc73-08e310482454" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + \\sec^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168608}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d4363fdf-f405-59b8-b8f3-f8bb7ba8806f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168603}] :question/id #uuid "f58568c3-ce47-5162-ad81-da8c754e43d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-123439" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}ds$, where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r = \\sec^{3} θ$ for $- π/2 \\le θ \\le π/2$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5777905c-e581-5454-bdbe-29878ad1237b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals of Unity Over Polar Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45cba1d3-f8b7-5b76-9ef6-9eb975d2870d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387403} {:answer/id #uuid "95d593ff-808e-5dde-a3cc-592a9fac2bb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387404} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c4989d-33d6-5c4f-b18e-27c12c50a4bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\text{ }\\,\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387405} {:answer/id #uuid "79e5bff5-9c94-5976-bd20-8b6b92ba67c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387406} {:answer/id #uuid "319dbf5e-c635-5b66-abde-d87e62f4983b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79e5bff5-9c94-5976-bd20-8b6b92ba67c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387406} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b0a388b-3f2b-5b8c-b2b8-fb5efa3b5837" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6fb03672-f783-5453-94bf-11f27f84fe3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-123454" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta })^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3081ffe7-c448-5e0d-8dd1-808d23199a7f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34de18da-e1ca-5f8c-97df-2693296f5a3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2},\\,-\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387410} {:answer/id #uuid "aa27ce8e-bcaf-51a1-a29e-a8a4f48be71a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\sqrt{2}-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387411} {:answer/id #uuid "21af6a56-2edf-5642-9e1f-191738f36d45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,2-\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387412} {:answer/id #uuid "372e4110-4717-5d1d-9f56-6b19027a531c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387413} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2bfecb-fc50-5b9d-8572-2f9284f9bdf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2},\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa27ce8e-bcaf-51a1-a29e-a8a4f48be71a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\sqrt{2}-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387411} :answer-field/id #uuid "7066aadc-9ef8-5807-82c3-9874779befc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb8b3222-c045-5fd0-b252-08063208b15e" :question/math-academy-id "q-123455" :question/problem "${\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}⟨\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡t}{1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t},\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t=$" :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}⟨\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡t}{1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t},\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡t}{1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t,{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t){|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1,\\,\\sqrt{2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\sqrt{2}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3081ffe7-c448-5e0d-8dd1-808d23199a7f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2538f5ee-0281-5a1a-a459-bed4091a0a76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-64,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387417} {:answer/id #uuid "7e03c91b-66f5-588c-9ccf-58f2e4ae6b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-9,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387418} {:answer/id #uuid "de948485-fe65-52ba-8ecd-1d8186f332a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-45,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387419} {:answer/id #uuid "53e2d7ae-d4a1-5d86-902a-7fa6ed88764c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-25,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387420} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d66e58-61c0-5c35-a3c5-5c78e04d0734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-81,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de948485-fe65-52ba-8ecd-1d8186f332a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-45,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387419} :answer-field/id #uuid "f330db29-f695-5fe4-b17b-04f55ac4a611" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a75a9ac2-e5a8-56b8-90de-02ef8cc74ba8" :question/math-academy-id "q-123456" :question/problem "${\\int }_{4}^{9}⟨4-2t,\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}⟩\\text{d}t=$" :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}⟨4-2t,\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨{\\int }_{4}^{9}4-2t\\,\\text{d}t,{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(4t-{t}^{2})\\,\\,{|}_{4}^{9},\\,2\\sqrt{t}\\,\\,{|}_{4}^{9}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-45-0,\\,6-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-45,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8dd18a5b-d7a1-5590-ae70-f47ff5b464b1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Vector-Valued Function That Satisfies a Given Initial Condition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92bd46e9-466f-57be-9494-e62e4206863e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t-2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387424} {:answer/id #uuid "91e6b479-4d1d-50b9-a666-14bbe2a96288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+3,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387425} {:answer/id #uuid "2e96ac04-3ce4-5f49-a6c0-fa0cb8f3ad89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t-2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387426} {:answer/id #uuid "89265a3e-77c9-5ab8-97a7-b5f526b95221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{sec}^{2}⁡t-3,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387427} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd60ea0-d4d2-5019-8caa-a6326a1a658f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2t)-2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92bd46e9-466f-57be-9494-e62e4206863e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t-2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387424} :answer-field/id #uuid "4770b818-387a-5d61-851e-363bf9058c69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff7e196f-5947-5cda-8ba7-68642d1d79ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-123472" :question/problem "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩$ and $F(\\pi )=⟨-3,\\,3⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition $F(\\pi )=⟨-3,\\,3⟩$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\pi )=⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi )+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨-3,\\,3⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1+{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨-3,\\,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=-2$ and ${c}_{2}=3.$ Consequently\n\n$$\nF(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t-2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+3⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8dd18a5b-d7a1-5590-ae70-f47ff5b464b1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Vector-Valued Function That Satisfies a Given Initial Condition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bc51601-01b5-5faa-ab90-dc09f164e2c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5{t}^{3}-5,\\,4{({t}^{2}-1)}^{2}+4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387431} {:answer/id #uuid "3f8ba005-4cf2-5230-a6ea-8dd69379fb02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5{t}^{4}-4,\\,4{({t}^{2}-1)}^{2}+5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387432} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6e9ec1-cadd-5646-abe3-5e43f9e8a79c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5{t}^{4}+4,\\,4{({t}^{2}-1)}^{2}+5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387433} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9ea5bf-c38e-5b09-9a9e-9f083d455730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5{t}^{5}-4,\\,4{(t-1)}^{2}+5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387434} {:answer/id #uuid "14ccbeaa-8897-5ed9-b232-96f70c4f9750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{t}^{2}-4,\\,4{({t}^{4}-1)}^{2}-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f8ba005-4cf2-5230-a6ea-8dd69379fb02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5{t}^{4}-4,\\,4{({t}^{2}-1)}^{2}+5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387432} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d7b8aac-8633-5c20-b2e7-76e8d6a9bed7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbc5ca63-1df5-5c8f-890f-d266498c8163" :question/math-academy-id "q-123473" :question/problem "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨20{t}^{3},\\,16t({t}^{2}-1)⟩$ and $F(1)=⟨1,\\,5⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨20{t}^{3},\\,16t({t}^{2}-1)⟩\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨20{t}^{3},\\,16t({t}^{2}-1)⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int 20{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 16t({t}^{2}-1)\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5{t}^{4}+{c}_{1},\\,4{({t}^{2}-1)}^{2}+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe initial condition $F(1)=⟨1,\\,5⟩$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1)=⟨5\\cdot {1}^{4}+{c}_{1},\\,4{({1}^{2}-1)}^{2}+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨1,\\,5⟩ \\\\ ⟨5+{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨1,\\,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=-4$ and ${c}_{2}=5.$ Consequently\n\n$$\nF(t)=⟨5{t}^{4}-4,\\,4{({t}^{2}-1)}^{2}+5⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3081ffe7-c448-5e0d-8dd1-808d23199a7f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd09d351-ea33-52a5-87d0-46aa58ea991c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{{e}^{6}},\\,128⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387438} {:answer/id #uuid "63a8ece2-2d03-5c39-a1fb-7f43218af02c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6+\\frac{1}{e},\\,228⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387439} {:answer/id #uuid "1e2629ec-1612-52ad-bb7f-2741283ce992" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1-{e}^{3},\\,108⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387440} {:answer/id #uuid "45fd9c10-b9d7-5f10-afe9-9f3b1f8c21a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{6}},\\,288⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387441} {:answer/id #uuid "0a127340-86b6-5de9-8713-8f42e907f3a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}},\\,280⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45fd9c10-b9d7-5f10-afe9-9f3b1f8c21a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{6}},\\,288⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387441} :answer-field/id #uuid "181904b2-593b-5626-88f0-8d67e397d190" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8f25bbf-4e2b-5901-9f0f-91861655df78" :question/math-academy-id "q-123517" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{6}⟨{e}^{-t},\\,4{t}^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}t=$" :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}⟨{e}^{-t},\\,4{t}^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨{\\int }_{0}^{6}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t,{\\int }_{0}^{6}4{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(-{e}^{-t}){|}_{0}^{6},\\,\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{6}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{-6}+{e}^{-0},\\,\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {6}^{3}-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{6}},\\,288⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc11d5-8eda-52b1-b7b1-42b481813ff4" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3809196-0e72-568a-acb4-8f1806b011f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d1cec5ea2da4a0b5f141bb24b4addc797defbc48e76cdfdbfab3a303e348b8cc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387445} {:answer/id #uuid "d44a46da-960a-5000-b573-28968c62249a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bcb2b08bab14ce74347d37a0e896451750db0203ab830b835a83de6b7a81b61d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387446} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c8555e-f76d-58d2-98de-66b72a2089cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/02/02b248ea373f295fa4dbad215eba4c211ef18548dca4a56d57cb3f15424ad501.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387447} {:answer/id #uuid "6dbecde8-5a29-5731-8756-dcd4a93b4487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.ty